f july  when there were only 18 cases per 100  thousand but by the start of september a  sudden spike saw the area suffering  its highest seventh day rate of  infections  since late may and this meant  restrictions were increased  even further with a curfew on businesses  and no  indoor hospitality much like the  nationwide  measures announced today but there are  some scientists who believe  lockdowns are ineffective they think  vulnerable people  living in our communities should be  protected and then the virus should be  allowed  to run its natural course  one solution that is afforded by how  this virus behaves in the community  is really a simple one which is where we  allow herd immunity to accumulate  through natural infection among those  who are not vulnerable to death so if we  can  arrange for that to happen then  that is the simplest solution to the  problem  and that is not a pipe dream  that can be done by protecting  those who are vulnerable  it's a very difficult choice for a prime  minister trying to balance health needs  with the needs of the economy for now  he's listened to his advisers  who are urging restrictions but he has  not imposed a full  national lockdown if these measures work  he won't need to
 

